### Step 2b — Corrected version

```powershell
# Create the Sales file share
New-SmbShare -Name "Sales" -Path "D:\Shares\Sales" -FullAccess "CORP\Domain Admins" -ChangeAccess "CORP\Authenticated Users"

# Set NTFS permissions for the Sales group
icacls "D:\Shares\Sales" /inheritance:r
icacls "D:\Shares\Sales" /grant "CORP\Sales_Group:(OI)(CI)M"
icacls "D:\Shares\Sales" /grant "CORP\Domain Admins:(OI)(CI)F"

# Apply FSRM hard quota (explicitly hard — the default -Usage flag isn't guaranteed across cmdlet versions)
New-FsrmQuota -Path "D:\Shares\Sales" -Size 25GB -Usage

# Block ransomware-prone extensions
New-FsrmFileGroup -Name "Sales-Blocked-Extensions" -IncludePattern @("*.exe","*.js")
New-FsrmFileScreen -Path "D:\Shares\Sales" -IncludeGroup "Sales-Blocked-Extensions" -Active

# Create and configure the IIS site
Install-WindowsFeature -Name Web-Server -IncludeManagementTools
New-Item -Path "C:\inetpub\SalesPortal" -ItemType Directory -Force

# Generate (or import) a certificate and bind it to the port BEFORE the HTTPS binding is used —
# an https binding with no certificate bound at that IP:port will fail the TLS handshake.
$cert = New-SelfSignedCertificate -DnsName "salesportal.corp.company.local" -CertStoreLocation "Cert:\LocalMachine\My"
$thumbprint = $cert.Thumbprint

New-IISSite -Name "SalesPortal" -PhysicalPath "C:\inetpub\SalesPortal" -BindingInformation "*:443:"
New-WebBinding -Name "SalesPortal" -Protocol https -Port 443

# Bind the certificate to the port via netsh — this is the step New-WebBinding does NOT do for you
$appId = "{" + [guid]::NewGuid().ToString() + "}"
netsh http add sslcert ipport=0.0.0.0:443 certhash=$thumbprint appid=$appId
```

### Step 2c — Note on what was changed and why

The AI's script created an HTTPS binding with `New-WebBinding -Protocol https -Port 443`, but never associated an actual SSL certificate with that IP:port combination — `New-WebBinding` only registers the binding metadata in IIS, it does not perform the certificate-to-port association Windows needs at the HTTP.sys level. Without that, any client hitting the site over HTTPS would get a TLS handshake failure, even though the binding "exists." I fixed it by generating a certificate, then explicitly binding it with `netsh http add sslcert`, which is the step that actually makes HTTPS traffic work. I also tightened the FSRM quota call to explicitly pass `-Usage` (hard quota) rather than relying on the cmdlet's default behavior, since quota type should never be left implicit in production scripts.



